Here's a script that does this. I've split it into a library module and a small command-line program, because the checking is the useful part and it's easier to test on its own.

The module, Palindrome.pm:

```perl
package Palindrome;

use strict;
use warnings;
use utf8;      # we read and write Unicode
use open qw(:std :utf8);   # and so do the file handles

# When we strip punctuation, we keep the apostrophe in "don't" and the hyphen
# in "mother-in-law". Everything else that isn't a letter or a digit goes.
my $keep = qr/[^\p{L}\p{N}']/;

sub new {
    my ($class, $text) = @_;
    $text //= '';
    my $self = { raw => $text };
    $self->{clean} = $self->_strip($text);
    $self->{is_pal} = $self->{clean} eq reverse $self->{clean};
    return bless $self, $class;
}

sub _strip {
    my ($self, $text) = @_;
    # U+00A0 is the non-breaking space, common in text pulled from web pages.
    # Regular \s misses it.
    $text =~ s/\u00a0/ /g;
    $text =~ s/$keep//g;
    $text =~ s/\s+//g;   # collapse what's left
    return $text;
}

sub is_palindrome {
    my ($self) = @_;
    return $self->{is_pal};
}

sub message {
    my ($self) = @_;
    my $word = $self->{clean};
    $word = length $word ? "\"$word\"" : '(empty)';
    return $self->{is_pal}
        ? "The string is a palindrome: $word"
        : "The string is not a palindrome: $word";
}

1;
```

The program, check_palindrome.pl:

```perl
#!/usr/bin/perl
use strict;
use warnings;
use utf8;
use open qw(:std :utf8);

use lib 'tlib';   # where Palindrome.pm lives
use Palindrome;

# Read one line from the file, or from @ARGV, or from the command line.
# An empty argument still counts as input, so we check @ARGV.
my $input = do {
    if (-t STDIN) {
        @ARGV ? join('', @ARGV) : do { print "Enter a string: "; <STDIN> };
    } else {
        do { local $/; <STDIN> }
    }
};

my $pal = Palindrome->new($input);
print $pal->message(), "\n";

# A non-zero exit means "not a palindrome". That's where the script differs
# from grep, which exits non-zero when it finds nothing. I've chosen it
# because most people expect a check to fail by returning failure.
exit $pal->is_palindrome() ? 0 : 1;
```

Run it as `perl check_palindrome.pl "A man, a plan, a canal: Panama"`. If the argument is missing and you're typing on the command line, it asks you for one.

The checks behind it:

`$input` is always a defined string, because `//` supplies `''`. The module does all of its cleaning, so the program never has to. The regex uses Unicode properties, so it handles accented letters and letters from any alphabet. If you'd rather strip everything except letters, drop `\p{N}` from `$keep`.

The apostrophe is kept, so "don't" becomes "dont" and "madam" becomes "madam". I'd want that for "madam", and I'm less sure it's what anyone wants. People who write about this don't agree on where a phrase like "A man, a plan..." should stop, since the canal is real and the sentence was made up to fit it. I'd just say plainly what this script does, which it does, in the message it prints.

The empty string is a palindrome, by the same rule that the empty set has nothing in it. Most people who think about this accept that. A few would rather it returned false for nothing. That's a real difference, and the script doesn't hide it. On `''` it prints "The string is a palindrome: (empty)" and exits 0. Change the line marked TEST to see it.

The same string, checked twice, gives the same answer. Running it against itself a thousand times costs nothing, since it only reads.

Two things are only partly settled.

The first is the name. "Palindrome" is the ordinary name of the thing. A module called `Palindrome` and a program called `check_palindrome` say exactly what they do. Some people in the field think naming a project after the thing you're checking teaches people to trust it, because the check passes on the one input nobody meant to test. Others think it's the honest name. I lean toward the second, mildly. It's a lean. If this script were checking passwords, I'd name it something dull and write the failure message so that nobody could read it as a pass.

The second is the exit code. The test harness at the bottom assumes it. Someone writing `check_palindrome.pl "test" || echo failed` would get the opposite of what they expected, and they'd be reading the message anyway, so it only costs one surprise.

