Here's the implementation. I've written it as a class with the matching logic separated out, so the program is easy to test and easy to change.

```java
import java.util.regex.*;
import java.io.*;
import java.util.*;

public class LineMatcher {

    private static final String PROMPT_TEXT =
            "Enter a string (or paste multiple lines, end with Ctrl-D / Ctrl-Z):\n" +
            "Enter a regular expression:\n";

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        try {
            System.out.print(PROMPT_TEXT);

            // Read the input string. We read line by line so the user
            // can paste several lines and press Enter at the end.
            StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
            while (scanner.hasNextLine()) {
                String line = scanner.nextLine();
                // A blank line at the end, typed by someone who was done,
                // gets dropped. That's most people. Someone who wanted
                // an empty string can type it, which leaves one line.
                if (sb.length() > 0 || !line.isEmpty()) {
                    sb.append(line).append('\n');
                }
                if (!scanner.hasNextLine()) break;
            }
            String input = sb.toString();

            // Read the pattern, trimming once at the outer edge,
            // because spaces inside [] are real.
            String pattern = scanner.nextLine().trim();

            if (pattern.isEmpty()) {
                throw new IllegalArgumentException("The regular expression cannot be empty.");
            }

            Pattern regex = Pattern.compile(pattern);

            int count = countMatchingLines(input, regex);

            System.out.println("\n--- Result ---");
            System.out.println("Input length: " + input.length() + " characters");
            System.out.println("Pattern: " + pattern);
            System.out.println("Lines matching: " + count);
            System.out.println("Total lines: " + (input.isEmpty() ? 0 : input.split("\n", -1).length));

        } catch (PatternSyntaxException e) {
            error("Invalid regular expression", e, e.getMessage());
        } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
            error(e.getMessage(), null, null);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            error("An unexpected error occurred", e, e.getMessage());
        }
    }

    private static void error(String title, Throwable cause, String message) {
        System.err.println(title + ":");
        System.err.println(message != null ? message : cause.getMessage());
        if (cause != null) {
            cause.printStackTrace(System.err);
        }
    }

    /**
     * Counts the lines in text that match the pattern.
     * Empty lines count as a line that doesn't match.
     * A pattern with no capturing groups uses the whole line.
     */
    public static int countMatchingLines(String text, Pattern pattern) {
        if (text.isEmpty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        String[] lines = text.split("\n", -1);

        // When a pattern has groups, find() walks through the matches
        // inside each line. That's a different count from lines.
        Matcher matcher = pattern.matcher(text);
        if (pattern.matcher("").lookingAt()) {
            // The pattern matches nothing at all, so no line matches it.
            return 0;
        }

        int count = 0;
        for (String line : lines) {
            if (matcher.reset(line).find()) {
                count++;
            }
        }

        return count;
    }
}
```

The split matters more than it looks. `String.split("\n")` drops trailing empty lines, so a file that ends with a blank line comes in short by one. `split("\n", -1)` keeps them, and so does the check inside `countMatchingLines` for a pattern that matches nothing. Without it, the count says more than it does.

The program also prints the total number of lines. A user who pastes text, types a pattern and sees "3" knows nothing unless they can check it. The total lets them.

